<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="phytochelatins biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6745" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: Poly-Gamma-Glutamylcysteine-Glycines Phytochelatins| are oligomers of glutathione that function as metal chelators and are vital for heavy metal detoxification by organisms |CITS: [19224399]|. They are widely distributed among living organisms including fungi, algae, nematodes and higher plants. Phytochelatins are formed by the action of the enzyme phytochelatin synthase |CITS: [21369921]|. The enzyme mechanism has been studied in detail and it appears that the substrate is glutathione molecules with blocked thiol groups. Increase in the amount of heavy metals leads to glutathione being blocked, which in turn results in increased phytochelatin synthase activity in the cells |CITS: [19067014][19017114 ]|. In higher plants the phytochelatins are transported to the vacuoles for the sequestering of metal ions away from proteins in the cytosol.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="nilo-poyanco" />
